.TH CHTTPCLIENT_DO_STREAMING 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_do_streaming \- do an HTTP request and stream the response body
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpclient_do_streaming(chttpcli cli,
                                       const chttp_request_t *req,
                                       chttpcli_write_fn write_fn,
                                       void *write_ctx,
                                       int *status_code_out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function works like chttpclient_do. But it gives the response
body to write_fn in chunks while the data arrives, and it does not
buffer that body. It allocates no chttpcli_response struct. This path
gives you no access to the response headers.

write_fn must give back the number of bytes that it consumes. A value
other than len aborts the transfer with a failure.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli             The client handle. It must not be CHTTPCLI_INVALID.
req             The request to run. It must not be NULL.
write_fn        The callback that the client calls with each chunk of
                the body. It must not be NULL.
write_ctx       An opaque context pointer. The client gives it to
                write_fn without a change.
status_code_out It gets the HTTP status code after a call that
                succeeds. It can be NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The same codes as chttpclient_do, with two differences. The function
never gives ccol_msg_too_large, because
chttpclient_set_max_response_body_size has no effect on this streaming
path. The write_fn of the caller already controls the use of memory
there. The function also gives ccol_http_transfer_aborted when write_fn
gives back a value other than len. Such a value aborts the transfer,
and it covers a value above len as well as a value below len.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpclient_do (3),
.BR chttpclient_do_async_streaming (3),
.BR chttpclient_do_pooled_streaming (3)
